# Config Examples

A step-by-step tutorial, from a meta-history dump to the Revision API, with runnable example
classes is in [dkpro-jwpl-tutorial/README.md](../dkpro-jwpl-tutorial/README.md).

## Diff Tool Config

```xml
<config>
	<values>
		<VALUE_MINIMUM_LONGEST_COMMON_SUBSTRING>12</VALUE_MINIMUM_LONGEST_COMMON_SUBSTRING>
		<COUNTER_FULL_REVISION>1000</COUNTER_FULL_REVISION>
	</values>
	<externals>
		<sevenzip>"D:\Programme\Utilities\7-Zip\7z.exe"</sevenzip>
	</externals>
	<input>
		<MODE_SURROGATES>DISCARD_REVISION</MODE_SURROGATES>
		<WIKIPEDIA_ENCODING>UTF-8</WIKIPEDIA_ENCODING>
		<archive>
			<type>SEVENZIP</type>
			<path>"D:\simplewiki.7z"</path>
			<start>0</start>
		</archive>
	</input>
	<output>
		<OUTPUT_MODE>UNCOMPRESSED</OUTPUT_MODE>
		<PATH>""</PATH>
		<LIMIT_SQL_FILE_SIZE>1000000000</LIMIT_SQL_FILE_SIZE>
		<MODE_ZIP_COMPRESSION_ENABLED>true</MODE_ZIP_COMPRESSION_ENABLED>
		<MODE_DATAFILE_OUTPUT>false</MODE_DATAFILE_OUTPUT>
	</output>
	<cache>
		<LIMIT_TASK_SIZE_REVISIONS>5000000</LIMIT_TASK_SIZE_REVISIONS>
		<LIMIT_TASK_SIZE_DIFFS>1000000</LIMIT_TASK_SIZE_DIFFS>
		<LIMIT_SQLSERVER_MAX_ALLOWED_PACKET>1000000</LIMIT_SQLSERVER_MAX_ALLOWED_PACKET>
		<!-- Optional: number of archives converted in parallel (default: 1) -->
		<LIMIT_ARCHIVE_THREADS>1</LIMIT_ARCHIVE_THREADS>
	</cache>
	<logging>
		<root_folder>""</root_folder>
		<diff_tool>
			<level>INFO</level>
		</diff_tool>
	</logging>
	<debug>
		<verification_diff>false</verification_diff>
		<verification_encoding>false</verification_encoding>
		<statistical_output>false</statistical_output>
		<debug_output>
			<enabled>false</enabled>
			<path>""</path>
		</debug_output>
	</debug>
	<filter>
		<namespaces>
			<!-- White list of namespaces; if nothing here then all namespaces are allowed -->
			<ns>0</ns>
			<ns>1</ns>
		</namespaces>
	</filter>
</config>

```

### Converting several archives in parallel

Full-history dumps of large Wikipedias are split into many archives
(`pages-meta-history1.xml-p1p812.bz2`, ...). List all of them as `<archive>`
entries and set `LIMIT_ARCHIVE_THREADS` to the number of archives that should
be converted at the same time. By default (`1`) the archives are converted one
after another on a single thread and written to the same output files
(`output_1.sql`, `output_2.sql`, ...).

With a value greater than `1`, every archive is converted by its own worker
and written to its own output files, named after the archive:
`pages-meta-history1.xml-p1p812.bz2` becomes
`output_pages-meta-history1.xml-p1p812_1.sql.bz2` (and `_2`, `_3`, ... once
`LIMIT_SQL_ARCHIVE_SIZE` / `LIMIT_SQL_FILE_SIZE` is reached). The file names
and their content therefore do not depend on the order in which the workers
finish, and the rows are the same as those of a single-threaded run. Each
worker keeps its own article task in memory, so size the heap for
`LIMIT_ARCHIVE_THREADS` times the task limits above. The `DATABASE` output
mode writes over a single connection and always runs on one thread.

## Index Generator Config

```
host=localhost
db=wiki_en_20110405_rev
user=student
password=student
output=target
outputDatabase=false
outputDatafile=false
charset=UTF8
buffer=15000
maxAllowedPackets=16760832
```

## Indexes on the revisions table

The DiffTool declares the secondary indexes
`articleIdx (ArticleID, RevisionCounter)` and
`articleTsIdx (ArticleID, Timestamp, RevisionCounter)` when it creates the
`revisions` table and builds both in one pass after the bulk load. Declaring them up front matters for MyISAM: every later `CREATE INDEX`
copies the whole table, diff payloads included. After the index tables have
been generated, the index generator enables the keys of the `revisions` table
and, for tables created by earlier versions, creates the following indexes if
they are missing:

```sql
CREATE INDEX articleIdx ON revisions(ArticleID, RevisionCounter);
CREATE INDEX articleTsIdx ON revisions(ArticleID, Timestamp, RevisionCounter);
```

The composite index `articleTsIdx` allows timestamp-based lookups of an article
(e.g. `getRevisionTimestamps(...)` or `getRevision(articleID, Timestamp)`) to be
answered from the index instead of reading every revision row of the article.

Revision databases created with an earlier version can be migrated with the
following statement (on large MyISAM tables this rebuilds the index file and
may take several hours):

```sql
ALTER TABLE revisions ADD INDEX articleTsIdx (ArticleID, Timestamp, RevisionCounter);
```
